CONFLICTS_MCQS = [
    # OPERATION SINDOOR & INDIA-PAKISTAN 2025 (22001-22016)
    {"id":22001,"question_text":"On which date did India launch 'Operation Sindoor' against terrorist camps in Pakistan and PoK?","option_a":"May 7, 2025","option_b":"April 22, 2025","option_c":"May 15, 2025","option_d":"March 7, 2025","correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Operation Sindoor was launched on May 7, 2025 — India's precision missile strikes on 9 terrorist camps across Pakistan and Pakistan-Occupied Kashmir, in retaliation for the Pahalgam terror attack (April 22, 2025 that killed 26 tourists). The operation marked India's first deep strike inside Pakistan proper since the 1971 Bangladesh War, signaling a strategic shift toward 'preemptive counter-terrorism.' Operationally, the 23-minute strike used precision missiles targeting JeM (Jaish-e-Mohammed) and LeT (Lashkar-e-Taiba) infrastructure in Punjab and PoK. Diplomatically, this triggered Pakistan's Operation Bunyan-um-Marsoos (May 10 retaliation), resulting in a 3-day standoff before US Secretary of State Rubio brokered a ceasefire at 5 PM IST on May 10. The operation raised international law questions over the right of self-defense under UN Charter Article 51 vs Pakistan's sovereignty claims over PoK.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22002,"question_text":"The Pahalgam terror attack (April 22, 2025) occurred at which specific location in Jammu & Kashmir?","option_a":"Dal Lake, Srinagar","option_b":"Baisaran Valley, Anantnag district","option_c":"Gulmarg ski resort","option_d":"Betaab Valley, Pahalgam","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The Pahalgam terror attack occurred at Baisaran Valley (a meadow near Pahalgam) in the Anantnag district of J&K on April 22, 2025, killing 26 tourists (mostly from Kashmir Valley hiking) and wounding 20+. TRF (The Resistance Front—a Lashkar-e-Taiba proxy formed in 2019) claimed responsibility, marking the deadliest terrorist attack on Indian civilians since the 2008 Mumbai attacks (166 killed). The attack targeted civilian tourists rather than military/security personnel, indicating a shift toward mass-casualty civilian terrorism designed to provoke India militarily. Domestically, the attack shattered Kashmir's nascent tourism recovery (J&K had ~17 million arrivals in 2024 post-Article 370). Pakistan-based LeT's strategic calculus appeared to force a military confrontation that would weaken Modi government's international standing. The attack violated the International Convention for the Suppression of Terrorist Bombings and UN Resolution 2625 on non-use of force. This triggered Operation Sindoor on May 7, 2025.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22003,"question_text":"How many tourists were killed in the Pahalgam terror attack of April 22, 2025?","option_a":"12","option_b":"18","option_c":"26","option_d":"33","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"26 tourists were killed in the Pahalgam terror attack (April 22, 2025) at Baisaran Valley, Anantnag, J&K — the worst terrorist attack on civilians in India since 2008 Mumbai attacks (166 killed, 166 wounded). An additional 20+ were injured, including locals and guides. The attack was linked to Pakistan-based Lashkar-e-Taiba (through its proxy TRF, formed Dec 2019 post-Article 370). LeT, founded in 1990 by Hafiz Saeed (US-designated terrorist), operates from Punjab province and PoK with ISI patronage. The attack violated international humanitarian law by deliberately targeting civilians. Within India's security doctrine, it demonstrated that despite Kashmir's internet closure (2019-2024) and counterinsurgency operations, cross-border militant infiltration remained uncontrolled—a major intelligence failure given 400+ LoC surveillance posts. The economic impact: Kashmir tourism receipts fell 37% from April-May 2025 as foreign tourist cancellations surged. This marked the 47th major cross-border terror attack on India since the 2019 Pulwama attack (40 CRPF killed).","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22004,"question_text":"Operation Sindoor targeted 9 terrorist camps. Which two Pakistan-based militant groups' infrastructure was specifically targeted?","option_a":"Al-Qaeda and ISIS","option_b":"Jaish-e-Mohammed and Lashkar-e-Taiba","option_c":"Haqqani Network and TTP","option_d":"LeT and Al-Badr","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Operation Sindoor (May 7, 2025) targeted infrastructure of Jaish-e-Mohammed (JeM) and Lashkar-e-Taiba (LeT) — Pakistan's two most dangerous US-designated terrorist organizations. JeM (founded 1997, led by Maulana Masood Azhar, HQ Bahawalpur) and LeT (founded 1990 by Hafiz Saeed, HQ Muridke) are intertwined with Pakistan's ISI and form the backbone of Kashmir-focused insurgency. Nine sites were struck across Pakistan proper (Punjab) and Pakistan-Occupied Kashmir using precision missiles (likely Brahmos or Nirbhay variants) by the Indian Air Force, lasting 23 minutes. The targets included command centers, ammunition dumps, and training facilities. The strikes demonstrated India's intelligence capability (real-time targeting) and precision-strike doctrine unprecedented in South Asian conflicts. India's stated objective was to degrade terrorist capacity, not invade or occupy—a line India repeatedly emphasized despite Pakistani allegations of escalation. Militarily, the operation employed standoff ranges to avoid PAF interception, indicating India's technological edge. Diplomatically, India justified the strikes under UN Article 51 (self-defense against cross-border terrorism), citing the pattern of attacks since 2019 Pulwama. India's 1992 maritime blockade of Pakistan (Operation Brasstacks) and 2019 Balakot strikes provided precedent, though Sindoor was deeper and more comprehensive.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22005,"question_text":"Pakistan's retaliatory operation against India (launched May 10, 2025) was codenamed:","option_a":"Operation Bunyan-um-Marsoos","option_b":"Operation Thunder Shield","option_c":"Operation Black Eagle","option_d":"Operation Iron Sword","correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Pakistan launched 'Operation Bunyan-um-Marsoos' on May 10, 2025 — a retaliatory operation after India's Operation Sindoor (May 7). Military hostilities continued for 3 days until a ceasefire was brokered at 5:00 PM IST on May 10, 2025. 'Bunyan-um-Marsoos' (Arabic: 'solid structure/wall,' from Surah Al-Kahf, Quranic verse 98) carries Islamic ideological significance, positioning Pakistan's response as a defensive rampart. Pakistan's military response involved PAF sorties, cruise missile strikes on Indian military installations, and ground operations along the LoC. However, Pakistan's operational response was constrained by: (a) PAF technical inferiority (India's Su-30MKI vs Pakistan's F-16, JF-17); (b) lack of air superiority; (c) fear of escalation to nuclear exchange given Pakistan's 180+ nuclear warheads. Pakistan's cost-benefit calculus appeared to favor ceasefire after the first day. Diplomatically, Pakistan's narrative emphasized Indian 'aggression' while claiming 'deterred' Indian ambitions. China and Russia expressed concern. The ceasefire reflected the nuclear stalemate between two nuclearized powers with historical territorial disputes (Kargil 1999, 2001-02 standoff). The International Institute for Strategic Studies later assessed Pakistan's response as 'constrained by strategic limitations.'","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22006,"question_text":"The India-Pakistan ceasefire of May 10, 2025 (following Operation Sindoor) was effective from what time?","option_a":"12:00 noon IST","option_b":"3:00 PM IST","option_c":"5:00 PM IST","option_d":"11:59 PM IST","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The India-Pakistan ceasefire after Operation Sindoor was effective from 5:00 PM IST / 4:30 PM PKT on May 10, 2025 — exactly 3 days after India's strikes. Pakistan acknowledged US Secretary of State Marco Rubio's diplomatic involvement in brokering the ceasefire through back-channel communications. Indian officials maintained the agreement was reached directly between the two countries (bilateral), minimizing US credit. The timing reflected nuclear escalation management: both sides assessed further strikes risked uncontrolled nuclear exchange. The ceasefire established a tense status quo ante bellum without addressing root causes (terrorist infiltration, Kashmir dispute, Indus Waters treaty suspension). International monitors from the UN Military Observer Group in India and Pakistan (UNMOGIP, established 1949) were activated. For diaspora communities, the ceasefire provided relief: Indian emigrant remittances (USD 119 billion annually) had threatened withdrawal amid war fears. The ceasefire's stability depended on Pakistan halting cross-border infiltration—a condition Pakistan neither accepted nor rejected formally. Regional stability implications: the ceasefire exposed India's military-strategic superiority while revealing Pakistan's constrained options in a nuclear-armed dyad.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22007,"question_text":"Operation Sindoor was India's precision strike response. How long did the initial operation last?","option_a":"5 minutes","option_b":"23 minutes","option_c":"1 hour 15 minutes","option_d":"4 hours","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Operation Sindoor lasted 23 minutes — a highly precise, rapid strike on 9 terrorist infrastructure targets in Pakistan (Punjab province + Lahore) and PoK. The tight timeframe (5 AM-5:23 AM IST) minimized Pakistan's air defense response windows and achieved surprise. Indian Air Force employed multiple sortie packages (likely Su-30MKI escorts, Brahmos-armed aircraft, and electronic warfare assets). The short duration reflected India's doctrine of 'sharp, swift, calibrated response' distinct from wider war. Militarily, the 23-minute window allowed India to complete strikes, withdraw aircraft, and establish air defense posture before PAF mobilization—a critical operational achievement given Pakistan's ~5-minute scramble response capability. The operation was designed to minimize civilian casualties through standoff missile strikes vs. penetrating bombing runs—adhering to International Humanitarian Law principles of distinction (military vs. civilian targets) and proportionality (military advantage vs. expected civilian harm). However, reports indicated 45-80 civilians killed in the broader military operations during the 3-day standoff. Pakistan later alleged India struck non-military sites, disputing India's 'surgical precision' narrative. The short duration also served psychological messaging: demonstrating India's capacity for rapid, deep strikes while maintaining strategic ambiguity about escalation intentions.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22008,"question_text":"After Operation Sindoor, India took several diplomatic measures against Pakistan. Which of the following is correct?","option_a":"India closed the Wagah-Attari border permanently","option_b":"India suspended the Indus Waters Treaty with Pakistan","option_c":"India expelled the Pakistani High Commissioner","option_d":"India banned all Pakistani goods imports and suspended the Indus Waters Treaty","correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Following the Pahalgam attack and Operation Sindoor, India adopted a comprehensive economic-diplomatic embargo against Pakistan: suspended the Indus Waters Treaty (1960, brokered by World Bank—a shared river governance framework surviving 3 wars and 65 years), banned all Pakistani goods imports, halted SAARC Visa Exemption Scheme for Pakistani nationals, and expelled the Pakistani High Commissioner. India also closed Indian airspace to Pakistani airlines (grounding Pakistan International Airlines, Air Blue operations). These measures targeted Pakistan's 2024 GDP (~USD 350B) and foreign exchange reserves (~USD 8B). The Indus Waters Treaty suspension was historically significant: it had survived the 1965, 1971, and 1999 wars and allocated water to Pakistan from western rivers (Indus, Jhelum, Chenab—total 93% of annual flow). Suspension threatened water security for Pakistan's 240 million population and 50% agricultural economy. India's rationale cited Pakistan's 'harboring of terrorists'—invoking the doctrine that national security justifies extraordinary measures. The visa ban restricted Pakistani student/family diaspora connections, affecting ~2.5M Pakistanis with Indian relatives. Economically, the ban impacted Pakistan's agricultural exports (cotton, rice, citrus). Diplomatically, the suspension violated the treaty's Article XII (dispute resolution at World Bank level), marking India's first unilateral treaty suspension since independence.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22009,"question_text":"The Pahalgam attack was attributed to which group — a proxy of Lashkar-e-Taiba?","option_a":"Al-Badr","option_b":"Hizbul Mujahideen","option_c":"The Resistance Front (TRF)","option_d":"Indian Mujahideen","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The Resistance Front (TRF) — a proxy of Lashkar-e-Taiba (LeT) — claimed responsibility for the Pahalgam terror attack (April 22, 2025) via encrypted Telegram and encrypted chat groups. TRF was formed in December 2019 immediately after the revocation of Article 370 (Jammu & Kashmir's special status) as LeT's 'indigenous' facade to claim Kashmir authorship and distance LeT from direct attacks—a propaganda strategy. TRF uses digital platforms (Telegram, encrypted chats, encrypted video platforms) for radicalization and operational claims, targeting Kashmiri youth aged 15-35. LeT (Lashkar-e-Taiba), founded in 1990 by Hafiz Saeed (designated terrorist by US, UN, EU) with ISI patronage, operates from Punjab and PoK with estimated 500-1,200 active operatives. LeT is the primary terror organization targeting India and is responsible for 2,000+ deaths since 1990. The attack reflected TRF's evolving targeting—from military/security personnel (earlier attacks) to mass-casualty civilian terrorism (Pahalgam). This shift indicated: (a) strategic intent to provoke military reaction that would internationalize Kashmir; (b) degraded Pakistan control over militant cells; (c) adaptation to Indian security improvements. TRF's designation as a terrorist organization by India (April 2022) and Pakistan (July 2024, under international pressure) underscored its transnational threat nexus. The attack's timing—during Kashmir's tourism season recovery—suggested targeting economic destabilization alongside casualty infliction.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22010,"question_text":"Operation Sindoor targeted terrorist camps in which two regions of Pakistan?","option_a":"Pakistan and Pakistan-Occupied Kashmir (PoK)","option_b":"Pakistan and Balochistan","option_c":"Sindh and Balochistan","option_d":"KPK and FATA","correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Operation Sindoor struck 9 terrorist camps across Pakistan (Punjab province) and Pakistan-Occupied Kashmir (PoK), specifically targeting: Bahawalpur (JeM headquarters, established 1990 with HQ in a fortified compound), Muridke (LeT headquarters, 30 km from Lahore, described as a 'university of terror'), and staging areas in Poonch/Muzaffarabad (PoK). The deep penetration (200+ km into Pakistan proper) marked a significant escalation from previous Indian counter-terrorism operations: the 2019 Balakot airstrikes (deep in Khyber-Pakhtunkhwa) and the 1999 Kargil War. This was the first time since the 1971 Bangladesh War that India conducted sustained strike operations targeting specific enemy leadership infrastructure. Strategically, the operation targeted the operational centers of Kashmir insurgency—denying space for infiltration planning, weapons storage, and personnel training. The PoK strikes implicitly challenged Pakistan's sovereignty claims, adding territorial dispute dimensions to counter-terrorism justifications. From a military geography perspective, the operation shifted India's doctrine from reactive (counter-insurgency in Kashmir) to active-preemptive (deep strikes on enemy rear). For regional powers, the operation signaled India's emerging capability to conduct deep strikes without full-scale war—a capability previously confined to major powers (US, Russia, China). Civilian casualties reports (45-80 killed) raised International Humanitarian Law concerns about proportionality and distinction principles.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22011,"question_text":"The SCO Summit 2025 in Tianjin explicitly condemned which terrorism incident — marking a diplomatic win for India?","option_a":"Uri attack 2016","option_b":"Pulwama attack 2019","option_c":"Pahalgam attack April 22, 2025","option_d":"Parliament attack 2001","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The Shanghai Cooperation Organization (SCO) Heads of State Summit in Tianjin (August-September 2025) issued an explicit joint declaration condemning the Pahalgam terror attack (April 22, 2025) and called for bringing perpetrators to justice. This was a significant diplomatic victory for India because it required consensus from all SCO members—notably Pakistan and China both joined the condemnation, isolating Pakistan's position and legitimizing India's Operation Sindoor response. The SCO (founded 2001, headquartered Shanghai; members: Russia, China, Kazakhstan, Kyrgyzstan, Tajikistan, Uzbekistan, India, Pakistan) had previously avoided direct condemnations of specific terror attacks due to Pakistan's blocking. The Pahalgam precedent broke this pattern, suggesting Pakistan's diplomatic position had weakened post-Operation Sindoor. India had held the SCO Chair in 2022-2023, securing terrorism denunciations against Pakistan. The 2025 Tianjin declaration reinforced India's counterterrorism narrative and delegitimized Pakistan's harbor of militant groups. For India's regional diplomacy, the SCO consensus provided multilateral cover for the Indus Waters Treaty suspension and armed retaliation. China's support suggested Beijing's concern over cross-border terrorism affecting Xinjiang and Pakistan-China Economic Corridor (CPEC) operations. The declaration also underscored SCO's anti-terrorism mandate (Article 4 of Charter) and justified India-Pakistan military posturing as counterterrorism rather than interstate aggression—critical for managing UN Security Council responses.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22012,"question_text":"India suspended which major water-sharing treaty with Pakistan after the Pahalgam attack 2025?","option_a":"Simla Agreement","option_b":"Lahore Declaration","option_c":"Indus Waters Treaty","option_d":"Tashkent Agreement","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"India suspended the Indus Waters Treaty (IWT, 1960)—the world's largest and longest-standing trans-boundary water agreement—after the Pahalgam attack. The IWT, brokered by the World Bank and signed by Indian FM Pandit Nehru and Pakistani PM Feroz Khan Noon, allocates waters from the Indus River system: Pakistan rights to western rivers (Indus, Jhelum, Chenab—total 81 million acre-feet annually) and India to eastern rivers (Sutlej, Beas, Ravi—33 million acre-feet). The treaty had survived 3 wars (1948, 1965, 1971), the 1999 Kargil War, and multiple terrorism incidents—testament to its resilience. Pakistan's water security depends on IWT waters for 95% of its irrigation (40 million hectares); suspension threatened agriculture, food security, and hydropower (40% of Pakistan's energy). India's suspension (May 16, 2025) invoked Article I(c) (modification to works) and implied future withdrawal under Article VII (arbitration). Legally, India attempted to remain within treaty bounds by claiming 'interim measures' while Pakistan argued suspension violated international water law principles (Helsinki Rules, UN Convention on Non-Navigational Uses of International Watercourses, customary international law). Pakistan approached the World Bank for arbitration (Treaty Article XII). The suspension's economic impact: Pakistan's water availability decreased 7-12% depending on season; agricultural GDP fell 3-4% by October 2025. For India, suspension served as leverage: any terrorist attack threatening suspension of water supplies constituted a strategic escalation threshold. Diplomatically, the suspension signaled India's willingness to overturn 65 years of institutional cooperation—the most drastic measure short of military conquest of water sources (Ladakh area dams on Indus tributaries).","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22013,"question_text":"What was India's code name for the Dec 2025-Jan 2026 evacuation of approximately 10,000 Indian nationals from Iran during civil unrest and economic collapse?","option_a":"Operation Sindhu","option_b":"Operation Swades","option_c":"Operation Sagar Bandhu","option_d":"Operation Ajay","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Operation Swades — launched Dec 2025-Jan 2026 — evacuated ~10,000 Indians from Iran amid mass civil unrest (Iran rial in free-fall, inflation 42%) preceding Operation Epic Fury (Feb 28, 2026). IAF C-17/C-130J + INS naval ships at Chabahar/Bandar Abbas + chartered flights were used. Sequence of evacuation ops: Ganga (Ukraine 2022), Kaveri (Sudan 2023), Ajay (Israel 2023), Sindhu (Iran June 2025), Sagar Bandhu (Sri Lanka Nov 2025), Swades (Iran 2025-26).","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22014,"question_text":"After Operation Sindoor, which US official was reportedly involved in brokering the India-Pakistan ceasefire of May 10, 2025?","option_a":"Secretary of State Marco Rubio","option_b":"National Security Advisor","option_c":"Vice President JD Vance","option_d":"CIA Director","correct_answer":"A","explanation":"US Secretary of State Marco Rubio was reportedly involved in diplomatic communications around the India-Pakistan ceasefire (May 10, 2025). Pakistan acknowledged American involvement; India maintained the ceasefire was reached bilaterally. Marco Rubio is Trump's Secretary of State (from January 20, 2025).","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22015,"question_text":"What was India's stated strategic objective of Operation Sindoor?","option_a":"Permanent occupation of PoK","option_b":"Destroy terrorist infrastructure and deter future attacks","option_c":"Regime change in Pakistan","option_d":"Capture of Gilgit-Baltistan","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"India's stated objective of Operation Sindoor: destroy terrorist infrastructure of JeM and LeT in Pakistan/PoK and deter future attacks. India emphasized it was NOT targeting Pakistani military or civilian infrastructure — only terrorist camps. The operation's name 'Sindoor' refers to the vermilion worn by married Hindu women — symbolic of the attack's victims.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22016,"question_text":"Which Swiss think tank reported that 'India's air superiority during Operation Sindoor forced Pakistan to seek ceasefire'?","option_a":"Geneva Centre for Security Policy","option_b":"International Institute for Strategic Studies","option_c":"Center for Security Studies (CSS) ETH Zürich","option_d":"Stockholm International Peace Research Institute","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The Center for Security Studies (CSS) at ETH Zürich (Swiss Federal Institute of Technology) published analysis that India's demonstrated air superiority during Operation Sindoor forced Pakistan to seek a ceasefire. This independent assessment supported India's claims of operational success in the 3-day India-Pakistan standoff.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},

    # TWELVE-DAY WAR (ISRAEL-IRAN JUNE 2025) (22017-22026)
    {"id":22017,"question_text":"The 'Twelve-Day War' between Israel and Iran took place during which period in 2025?","option_a":"March 1-12, 2025","option_b":"June 13-24, 2025","option_c":"August 5-16, 2025","option_d":"October 7-18, 2025","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The Twelve-Day War (Israel-Iran direct conflict, third escalation after Operation True Promise I and II) lasted June 13-24, 2025—a rapid militarized crisis spanning Middle East, Gulf region, and global oil markets. Israel launched a surprise attack on Iran's nuclear enrichment facilities (Natanz, Fordow, Isfahan) on June 13 following the IAEA Board of Governors' June 12 non-compliance resolution. Iran retaliated with Operation True Promise III (550+ ballistic missiles, 1,000+ suicide drones targeting Israeli cities, Al Udeid Air Base in Qatar, and allied bases). The US independently bombed 3 Iranian nuclear sites on June 22—the first direct US military action against Iran's nuclear infrastructure ever, fundamentally breaking the diplomatic pretense of 'pressure short of war.' The ceasefire on June 24, 2025 was achieved under US pressure (via Qatar, Egypt mediators) after global oil prices spiked to USD 140+/barrel. This conflict occurred DURING the active Nuclear Proliferation Treaty (NPT) regime—Iran had enriched uranium to 60% purity (near weapons-grade 90%), threatening a breakout within weeks. For India: the war disrupted oil supplies (India imports 70% crude from Middle East); Indian nationals faced evacuation risks (8 million Indians in Gulf states); India's Chabahar Port investment was threatened; Indian Navy monitored Hormuz Strait disruptions. Regionally, the war demonstrated the fragility of the post-2015 JCPOA order and Trump's maximalist pressure strategy. The conflict resulted in 1,000+ Iranian military casualties, 10+ nuclear scientists killed, and Iran's suspension of IAEA cooperation—effectively ending international nuclear monitoring for 6+ months.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22018,"question_text":"Which Israeli action on the day before the Twelve-Day War started was a significant trigger?","option_a":"Israel bombed Iranian oil refineries","option_b":"Israel struck Iran's consulate in Beirut","option_c":"IAEA declared Iran non-compliant with nuclear obligations (June 12, 2025)","option_d":"Israel blockaded Iranian shipping in Hormuz","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"On June 12, 2025 — the day before the Twelve-Day War — the IAEA Board of Governors adopted a resolution (sponsored by US, UK, France, Germany) declaring Iran non-compliant with its nuclear obligations. Israel used this as diplomatic cover to launch surprise attacks on Iranian nuclear sites on June 13, 2025.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22019,"question_text":"During the Twelve-Day War, which Iranian nuclear facilities were targeted by Israel?","option_a":"Arak, Qom, and Bushehr","option_b":"Natanz, Isfahan, and Fordow","option_c":"Tehran Research Reactor, Arak, and Khondab","option_d":"Bandar Abbas, Bushehr, and Chabahar","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Israel targeted three critical Iranian nuclear facilities during the Twelve-Day War: Natanz (70 km southeast of Isfahan, Iran's main uranium enrichment facility with 10,000+ IR-6 centrifuges enriching uranium to 60%), Isfahan (Uranium Conversion Facility + heavy water production, 400 km south of Tehran), Fordow (underground enrichment facility 180 km from Tehran, 100+ meters deep, thought unsinkable but damaged). The targets represented Iran's nuclear weapons-development pathway: conversion (Isfahan) → enrichment (Natanz, Fordow) → plutonium production (Arak heavy water reactor). Israel's targeting rationale: degrading Iran's breakout capability from 60% purity to 90% weapons-grade (estimated 3-4 weeks timeline before strikes). The US separately bombed 3 Iranian nuclear sites on June 22 (sites undisclosed, likely Arak, Bushehr, and one covert site). Technically, Israel used F-15 fighter-bombers and bunker-busting ordnance (GBU-57 Massive Ordnance Penetrator). After the war, Iran suspended IAEA (International Atomic Energy Agency) cooperation—expelling inspectors, halting monitoring, and removing cameras from enrichment sites. This created an 'opacity gap' in nuclear intelligence lasting 6+ months. From international law perspective, Israel's strikes raised questions under UN Charter Article 2(4) (prohibition on use of force) vs. Article 51 (self-defense). Iran's 60% enrichment violated the JCPOA (Joint Comprehensive Plan of Action, capped at 3.65%), justifying Israeli and US action under 'preventive war' doctrine. India maintained neutrality while securing Iranian assurances on oil supplies continuity post-ceasefire.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22020,"question_text":"Iran's retaliation during the Twelve-Day War was called 'Operation True Promise III.' How many ballistic missiles and drones were launched?","option_a":"100 missiles + 200 drones","option_b":"300 missiles + 500 drones","option_c":"550+ ballistic missiles + 1,000+ suicide drones","option_d":"200 missiles + 400 drones","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Iran's Operation True Promise III (Twelve-Day War, June 15-17, 2025 response window) involved 550+ ballistic missiles (Fateh, Zulfiqar, Khordad, Sejjil variants, range 2,000-2,600 km) + 1,000+ suicide drones (Shahed-136, Shahed-171 loitering munitions with 200+ km range). This represented the largest coordinated Iranian missile-drone attack ever. Targets included: Israeli cities (Tel Aviv, Jerusalem, Haifa—inflicting 600+ casualties), Ben-Gurion Airport, military bases, and strategically Al Udeid Air Base (Qatar, hosting US Air Forces Central Command). Iran's intent: demonstrate military capability to strike Israel and US assets simultaneously, deter further Israeli attacks, and raise war costs. Operationally, the volume (1,550+ projectiles) exploited Israeli air defense limits: Iron Dome (40-70 km range, limited inventory ~1,000 interceptors), David's Sling (100-300 km, limited), Arrow 2/3 (100-2,400 km, ~100 interceptors). Israel's defense required US Patriot interceptors and E-3 AWACS coordination. Strategically, True Promise III (third Iranian direct attack after True Promise I: April 2024 following Soleimani-style killing, True Promise II: October 2024) followed an escalation ladder established in 2019-2024. The operation marked Iran's attempt to 'normalize' direct strikes as part of its asymmetric deterrence doctrine. For regional stability: the scale demonstrated Iran's capacity for sustained conventional strikes (not nuclear), raising questions about escalation control mechanisms. For India: the operation tested Indian Ocean security (drones transited Arabian Sea); India monitored Hormuz Strait closure threats (20% global oil); Indian naval assets in Arabian Sea were on high alert.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22021,"question_text":"Iran struck which major US military base in Qatar during the Twelve-Day War?","option_a":"Camp Arifjan","option_b":"Al Udeid Air Base","option_c":"NSA Bahrain","option_d":"Camp As Sayliyah","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Iran struck Al Udeid Air Base in Qatar during the Twelve-Day War — the US military's largest air base in the Middle East, hosting the US Air Forces Central Command (AFCENT). Qatar hosts both US military and has historically maintained ties with Hamas. Al Udeid hosted ~10,000 US personnel.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22022,"question_text":"Which senior IRGC (Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps) commander was killed in the Twelve-Day War, 2025?","option_a":"Mohammad Bagheri only","option_b":"Hossein Salami only","option_c":"Hossein Salami (IRGC Commander), Amir Ali Hajizadeh (IRGC Aerospace), Mohammad Bagheri (Army Chief)","option_d":"Ali Larijani and Aziz Nasirzadeh","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Twelve-Day War (June 2025) killed multiple top Iranian military commanders: Hossein Salami (IRGC Commander-in-Chief), Amir Ali Hajizadeh (IRGC Aerospace Force Commander), Mohammad Bagheri (Armed Forces Chief of Staff), and Gholam Ali Rashid. 10+ nuclear scientists also killed. Masoud Pezeshkian (Iranian President) was wounded.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22023,"question_text":"How many Mossad (Israeli intelligence) agents were reported arrested by Iran following the Twelve-Day War?","option_a":"50","option_b":"150","option_c":"700+, with 5 executed","option_d":"1,200","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Iran arrested 700+ alleged Mossad agents following the Twelve-Day War, executing 5 of them. Iran also suspended IAEA cooperation after the war — the most severe action short of full withdrawal from the NPT (Non-Proliferation Treaty). Iran had been enriching uranium to 60% purity before the war.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22024,"question_text":"The Twelve-Day War ceasefire (June 24, 2025) was achieved under pressure from which country?","option_a":"Russia","option_b":"China","option_c":"United States","option_d":"Turkey","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The Twelve-Day War ceasefire (June 24, 2025) was achieved under US pressure. The US had itself bombed 3 Iranian nuclear sites on June 22 — the most direct US military action against Iran's nuclear program ever. Trump left G7 Kananaskis early (June 15-17) citing the need to deal with the conflict.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22025,"question_text":"What did Iran do to IAEA (International Atomic Energy Agency) cooperation after the Twelve-Day War?","option_a":"Expelled IAEA inspectors and suspended cooperation","option_b":"Invited IAEA for full inspection","option_c":"Agreed to halt all enrichment permanently","option_d":"Transferred nuclear materials to Russia","correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Iran suspended IAEA cooperation after the Twelve-Day War — expelling inspectors and halting all monitoring. Iran had been enriching uranium to 60% purity (near weapons-grade of 90%). The IAEA's June 12 resolution declaring Iran non-compliant had been the trigger for the war. The IAEA is headquartered in Vienna, Austria.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22026,"question_text":"Iranian President Masoud Pezeshkian was injured (WIA) during the Twelve-Day War. He became President of Iran in which year?","option_a":"2021","option_b":"2023","option_c":"2024","option_d":"2025","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Masoud Pezeshkian became President of Iran in July 2024, winning the presidential election after Ibrahim Raisi died in a helicopter crash in May 2024. Pezeshkian was a moderate reformist. He was wounded (WIA) during the Twelve-Day War (June 2025). Supreme Leader Ali Khamenei was subsequently killed in the 2026 Iran War (Feb 28, 2026).","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},

    # 2026 IRAN WAR (22027-22040)
    {"id":22027,"question_text":"The 2026 Iran War started on which date, with US and Israel launching coordinated strikes?","option_a":"January 15, 2026","option_b":"February 28, 2026","option_c":"March 15, 2026","option_d":"April 1, 2026","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The 2026 Iran War (Operation Epic Fury) began on February 28, 2026 when the Trump administration (through Pete Hegseth, Defense Secretary) and Israel (PM Netanyahu) launched coordinated strikes on Iran—the most consequential use of force in the region since 1991. The operation was explicitly timed DURING active Iran-US nuclear negotiations at the UN (February 26-27, 2026 session), creating diplomatic scandal: the US was negotiating while planning strikes, violating customary international law's requirement for good-faith bargaining. The operation decapitated Iran's leadership: Ali Khamenei (Supreme Leader, 37-year tenure since 1989), Ali Larijani (former Majlis Speaker, senior conservative), Abdolrahim Mousavi (Army Commander), Aziz Nasirzadeh (Air Force Commander), Alireza Tangsiri (IRGC Navy). Mojtaba Khamenei (son, potential successor) was wounded. The strikes also targeted: nuclear facilities (Arak, Bushehr, Qom complexes), missile production (IRGC Aerospace Force bases), command centers (IRGC HQ in Tehran), and leadership residences. The operation involved 2 US carrier strike groups, 150+ combat aircraft (F-22 Raptors, F-35 Lightning IIs, B-1 Lancers), precision-guided munitions, and possible cyber warfare elements. Iran responded by closing the Strait of Hormuz, striking US bases across the Gulf (Bahrain, Qatar, Kuwait, Jordan, Saudi Arabia), and launching asymmetric attacks via proxies (Hezbollah in Lebanon, Houthis in Yemen, Iraqi militias). The war lasted until April 8, 2026 ceasefire. For India: the war triggered oil price spikes (USD 140+/barrel), threatened 8 million Indian nationals in Gulf region, disrupted Chabahar Port operations (India's strategic gateway to Afghanistan/Central Asia), and exposed India's energy vulnerabilities—61% of oil from Middle East.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22028,"question_text":"Who was killed when the 2026 Iran War began with US-Israel coordinated strikes on February 28, 2026?","option_a":"Iranian President Pezeshkian","option_b":"Supreme Leader Ali Khamenei","option_c":"IRGC Commander Hossein Salami","option_d":"Hezbollah leader Naim Qassem","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Ali Khamenei — Iran's Supreme Leader since 1989 — was killed in the opening US-Israel strikes on February 28, 2026. Also killed: Ali Larijani (influential political figure), Abdolrahim Mousavi (Army Commander), Aziz Nasirzadeh (Air Force Commander), Alireza Tangsiri (IRGC Navy). Mojtaba Khamenei (son) was wounded.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22029,"question_text":"Iran's response to the 2026 War included closing which critical international waterway through which 20% of global oil passes?","option_a":"Suez Canal","option_b":"Bab el-Mandeb Strait","option_c":"Strait of Hormuz","option_d":"Strait of Malacca","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Iran closed the Strait of Hormuz (width 34 km at narrowest, between Iran's Musandam Peninsula and Oman) in response to Operation Epic Fury (February 28, 2026)—the world's most critical maritime chokepoint through which ~20% of global oil (17-18 million barrels/day, 6 billion barrels annually) and 20% of global LNG pass. The Strait connects the Persian Gulf (containing 48% of world's proven oil reserves: Saudi Arabia 16%, Iran 8%, UAE 6%, Kuwait 6%, Qatar 2%) to the Gulf of Oman and Arabian Sea. Iran's closure was implemented by: deploying IRGC Navy fast attack craft (Boushehr-class) to harass merchant shipping, laying mines, broadcasting navigational threats, and stationing missile batteries (C-802 anti-ship). Within 48 hours, global oil prices spiked from USD 90/barrel to USD 140+/barrel—the fastest spike since 1990 (Iraq invasion of Kuwait). For India: the closure was catastrophic—India imports 70% of its 4 million barrels/day from Middle East, with Hormuz transit serving 60% of Indian imports. Oil prices at USD 140 increased India's import bill by USD 20 billion annually (3.6% inflation impact). India's strategic reserve (Strategic Petroleum Reserve, 5.33 million barrel capacity, 50 days consumption) was activated. For global trade: closure added USD 200-400 per container for ships rerouting around Africa's Cape of Good Hope (10-14 extra days, 5,000+ nm additional voyage). Shipping insurance premiums in Hormuz doubled to 2-3% of cargo value. The closure lasted 39 days (until April 8 ceasefire) before US naval presence and diplomatic pressure (Pakistan mediation) reopened transit. Strategically, Iran's Hormuz closure was its most powerful conventional asymmetric weapon—costlier to global economy than military strikes.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22030,"question_text":"During the 2026 Iran War, Iran struck US military bases in multiple countries. Which of the following was NOT struck by Iran?","option_a":"Bahrain","option_b":"Jordan","option_c":"Egypt","option_d":"Kuwait","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Iran struck US military bases in: Bahrain (NSA Bahrain/5th Fleet HQ), Jordan (Al-Tanf area), Kuwait (Camp Arifjan), Qatar (Al Udeid), Saudi Arabia (Prince Sultan Air Base), and UAE (Al Dhafra). Egypt was NOT struck — Egypt hosts Sinai peacekeepers and maintains separate diplomatic relations. UK's Akrotiri base in Cyprus was also struck by drone.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22031,"question_text":"The 2026 Lebanon War ran parallel to the Iran War. How many Hezbollah fighters were killed in the 2026 Lebanon War?","option_a":"100-200","option_b":"500-800","option_c":"1,000-1,900","option_d":"3,000-4,000","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The 2026 Lebanon War (concurrent with 2026 Iran War) resulted in 1,000-1,900 Hezbollah fighters killed and 2,000+ total casualties. Hezbollah fired rockets at Israel from Lebanon. Israel struck Beirut again on May 7, 2026 — killing the then-current Hezbollah leader. Hezbollah SG Naim Qassem had taken over after Nasrallah's death in September 2024.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22032,"question_text":"Pakistan mediated the April 8, 2026 conditional Iran-US ceasefire and then hosted follow-up talks. When did the Pakistan-hosted US-Iran talks at Islamabad collapse?","option_a":"March 20-21, 2026","option_b":"April 11-12, 2026","option_c":"April 30, 2026","option_d":"May 7, 2026","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Pakistan (under PM Shehbaz Sharif, post-Operation Sindoor reputational recovery) brokered the April 8, 2026 'conditional ceasefire' between Iran and the US-Israel coalition, ending the active kinetic phase of the 2026 Iran War after 39 days and 15+ US soldiers killed, 3,636+ Iranians killed. The ceasefire was conditional: parties agreed to halt strikes, reopen Strait of Hormuz, and pursue negotiations. Pakistan's mediation role reflected: (a) Pakistan-Iran 900 km border and shared Shi'a-Sunni tensions; (b) Pakistan's interest in regional stability (energy imports, trade via Iran); (c) Pakistan's strategic desire to raise its profile post-Sindoor. Follow-up US-Iran negotiations at Islamabad (April 11-12, 2026 session) convened with US, Iran, Pakistan, and Qatar as participants. The talks collapsed over three irreconcilable demands: (a) Iran's demand for full US withdrawal from Persian Gulf bases (5th Fleet, CENTCOM forward HQ in Qatar, Al Udeid)—the US rejected, viewing these as core strategic architecture; (b) US insistence on permanent nuclear dismantlement (Arak reactor mothballing, centrifuge destruction, full IAEA access)—Iran rejected, viewing these as sovereignty violations; (c) Iran's reparations claim of USD 270-1 trillion for destroyed nuclear infrastructure, leadership casualties, economic damage—the US countered with USD 50-200 billion liability framework (US-flagged shipping damages, Gulf allies' losses). The collapse reflected the incompatibility of Iranian revolutionary ideology (resistance to US dominance) and US strategic interests (containing Iran's regional influence). Pakistan's mediation failure indicated even third-party powers lack leverage when core interests diverge. For India: the collapsed talks meant prolonged tensions in the Gulf (strategic interest), continued elevated oil prices (energy security), and uncertainty over India's positioning between Iran and US (diplomatic balancing act).","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22033,"question_text":"US casualties in the 2026 Iran War were:","option_a":"5 killed, 100 wounded","option_b":"15 killed, 538 wounded","option_c":"50 killed, 1,000 wounded","option_d":"100 killed, 2,000 wounded","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"US casualties in the 2026 Iran War: 15 soldiers killed, 538 wounded (as of ceasefire, April 8, 2026). Iran casualties (HRANA human rights group): 3,636 killed, 26,500 wounded; $270 billion to $1 trillion economic damage. Israel: 18 soldiers + 28 civilians killed; $50 billion economic damage.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22034,"question_text":"What was the scale of US military deployment during the 2026 Iran War — described as the largest Middle East buildup since 2003?","option_a":"1 aircraft carrier strike group","option_b":"2 aircraft carrier strike groups","option_c":"3 aircraft carrier strike groups","option_d":"Full Pacific Fleet deployment","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"US deployed 2 aircraft carrier strike groups for the 2026 Iran War — the largest Middle East military buildup since the 2003 Iraq invasion. US military leadership: Pete Hegseth (Defense Secretary), Michael Kurilla (CENTCOM Commander), Dan Caine (Joint Chiefs Chairman). US and Israel coordinated strikes starting February 28, 2026.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22035,"question_text":"Who was Israel's Defense Minister during the 2026 Iran War?","option_a":"Yoav Gallant","option_b":"Benny Gantz","option_c":"Israel Katz","option_d":"Avigdor Lieberman","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Israel's key leaders during the 2026 Iran War: PM Benjamin Netanyahu, Defense Minister Israel Katz, IDF Chief Eyal Zamir. US Defense Secretary: Pete Hegseth (appointed by Trump). Iran's Revolutionary Guard was severely weakened after Twelve-Day War (June 2025) commanders killed.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22036,"question_text":"Ali Khamenei had been Iran's Supreme Leader since which year?","option_a":"1979","option_b":"1985","option_c":"1989","option_d":"1993","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Ali Khamenei became Iran's Supreme Leader in 1989 following the death of Ayatollah Khomeini (the founder of Islamic Republic). Before becoming Supreme Leader, Khamenei was Iran's President (1981-1989). He held the Supreme Leader position for 37 years until killed in the 2026 Iran War (February 28, 2026).","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22037,"question_text":"The 2026 Iran War caused Iran to close the Strait of Hormuz. What percentage of global LNG (Liquefied Natural Gas) also passes through the Strait?","option_a":"5%","option_b":"10%","option_c":"20%","option_d":"30%","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The Strait of Hormuz carries ~20% of global oil AND approximately 20% of global LNG — including major exports from Qatar (world's top LNG exporter). Closure impacted global energy markets, driving oil prices above $100/barrel. India's Chabahar Port (in Iran) was also strategically affected.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22038,"question_text":"Iran's economic damage from the 2026 Iran War was estimated at:","option_a":"$10-50 billion","option_b":"$100-200 billion","option_c":"$270 billion to $1 trillion","option_d":"$5 trillion","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Iran's economic damage from the 2026 Iran War: $270 billion to $1 trillion (wide range due to ongoing conflict and estimation difficulties). Includes: infrastructure destruction, Strait of Hormuz closure economic impact, sanctions, oil revenue loss. Israel's damage: $50 billion. US deployed 2 carrier strike groups in the largest Middle East buildup since 2003 Iraq invasion.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22039,"question_text":"US Secretary of Defense Pete Hegseth was appointed under which president, and when did he take office?","option_a":"Trump, January 20, 2025","option_b":"Biden, January 2024","option_c":"Trump, November 2024","option_d":"Harris, January 2025","correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Pete Hegseth was appointed US Secretary of Defense by President Donald Trump, taking office January 20, 2025 (Trump's inauguration day). Hegseth was a Fox News host and Army National Guard veteran before becoming Defense Secretary. He led US military decisions during Operation Sindoor (India-Pakistan), the Twelve-Day War, and the 2026 Iran War.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22040,"question_text":"Israel struck Beirut on May 7, 2026 — killing a Hezbollah leader. Who had become Hezbollah's Secretary-General after Nasrallah was killed in September 2024?","option_a":"Hashem Safieddine","option_b":"Naim Qassem","option_c":"Ibrahim Aqil","option_d":"Fuad Shukr","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Naim Qassem became Hezbollah's Secretary-General after Hassan Nasrallah was killed in an Israeli airstrike on September 27, 2024. Qassem had been Hezbollah's Deputy Secretary-General for many years. He was killed in Israel's May 7, 2026 Beirut strike. The Hezbollah-Israel ceasefire of November 2024 broke down with the 2026 Lebanon War.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},

    # UKRAINE-RUSSIA WAR 2025-2026 (22041-22052)
    {"id":22041,"question_text":"In the Russia-Ukraine War, which country launched a historic surprise military incursion into Russia's Kursk Oblast in 2024, and what was its 2025 status?","option_a":"Ukraine incursed Kursk August 2024; Russian forces recaptured most by early 2025","option_b":"Russia incursed Ukraine's Sumy Oblast in 2024","option_c":"Belarus invaded Ukraine's Zhytomyr Oblast","option_d":"Ukraine launched missiles at Moscow in August 2024","correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Ukraine's surprise incursion into Russia's Kursk Oblast (August 6-9, 2024) — first time since WWII that foreign forces directly held Russian sovereign territory—was a strategic gambit by Ukraine's military (SBU Chief Budanov planning). Ukrainian forces (1st Tank Brigade, 22nd Mechanized Brigade) penetrated 40+ km into Kursk, capturing 1,200+ sq km and 15,000+ Russian POWs. The operation's strategic intent: (a) break the stalemate on Ukrainian soil; (b) demonstrate Ukrainian offensive capability (after years of Russian offensive); (c) create a 'bargaining chip' for future negotiations; (d) boost Ukrainian morale (first foreign invasion of Russia proper); (e) expose Russian mobilization deficiencies. By early 2025, Russian forces (with North Korean 10,000-12,000 troop reinforcements) had recaptured 60-70% of Kursk, though Ukraine retained 400-600 sq km as of May 2026. Militarily, the operation demonstrated Ukraine's tactical resourcefulness despite material disadvantage (outgunned 3:1 in artillery). Legally, the incursion raised questions under International Humanitarian Law: Ukraine's occupation of Russian territory (technically violating UN Charter Article 2(4)) was justified as counter-occupation response to Russian control of 20% of Ukrainian territory. Diplomatically, Kursk became leverage in Trump-mediated negotiations (January-May 2026), with Russia demanding Ukrainian withdrawal as ceasefire condition. For India: the Kursk operation revealed limitations of larger military forces against smaller, innovative adversaries—relevant to India-Pakistan asymmetric warfare doctrine. The operation's success rate (initial 40 km penetration before stabilization) contradicted pre-war assessments of Russia's overwhelming military dominance.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22042,"question_text":"North Korea sent approximately 10,000-12,000 troops to support Russia in the Ukraine war. How were these troops deployed?","option_a":"Defensive positions in Russia's western border","option_b":"Direct combat in Kursk Oblast against Ukrainian forces","option_c":"Logistics and supply chain support only","option_d":"Intelligence gathering in Belarus","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"North Korea (DPRK) deployed ~10,000-12,000 troops to Russia (October 2024 onwards) in direct combat roles in Kursk Oblast against Ukrainian forces, marking the first DPRK combat deployment outside Korean peninsula since 1950-53 Korean War. DPRK troops were primarily deployed in the 1st Guards Tank Army (western front against Ukraine's Kursk incursion). North Korea also supplied 3-5 million artillery shells (122mm, 152mm) and ballistic missiles (KN-02 Toksa, range 120 km), addressing Russia's critical ammunition deficit. Russia signed the Comprehensive Strategic Partnership Treaty with DPRK (October 2024), establishing mutual defense obligations (Article 4). Putin visited Pyongyang; Kim Jong-un visited Moscow (August 2023, then follow-ups). The deployment raised international law concerns: (a) UN Security Council prohibitions on DPRK foreign military activities; (b) potential violations of UN sanctions on DPRK; (c) expansion of DPRK's international combat experience (previously relegated to cyber warfare, proxy operations). Casualties: 1,500+ DPRK soldiers killed/wounded (first 3 months), exposing DPRK's poor combat training and outdated tactics. For India: DPRK-Russia cooperation indicated consolidation of anti-US axis (Russia, DPRK, Iran, China) against Western-aligned powers—relevant to India's strategic autonomy doctrine and China-Pakistan axis. The deployment also exposed DPRK's economic dependence on Russia (food, fuel shipments in return for soldiers + arms). The operation's personnel losses threatened DPRK's military regeneration capacity, suggesting potential instability in Korean peninsula (2026-2027).","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22043,"question_text":"Trump announced a 3-day Russia-Ukraine ceasefire in May 2026. What prisoner exchange was included?","option_a":"100 prisoners each","option_b":"500 prisoners each","option_c":"1,000 prisoners each","option_d":"All POWs simultaneously","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Trump's 3-day Russia-Ukraine ceasefire (May 9-11, 2026) included an exchange of 1,000 prisoners each side — a significant humanitarian element. Trump called it potentially 'the beginning of the end' of the war. US Secretary of State Rubio struck a somber tone, saying US mediation had not led to a 'fruitful outcome' and efforts had 'stagnated.'","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22044,"question_text":"Russia declared a unilateral ceasefire in Ukraine for May 8-9, 2026 to mark which occasion?","option_a":"Easter celebrations","option_b":"Russia's National Day","option_c":"World War II Victory Day","option_d":"Putin's birthday","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Russia declared a unilateral ceasefire for May 8-9, 2026 for its annual WWII Victory Day commemorations. Ukraine declared its own truce for May 5-6, calling Russia's ceasefire 'not serious' (overlap only on May 8). Trump then brokered the 3-day joint ceasefire (May 9-11). The WWII Victory Day (May 9) is Russia's most important national holiday.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22045,"question_text":"According to ACLED 2025 data, which conflict was the world's deadliest in 2025 with almost 78,000 fatalities?","option_a":"Sudan Civil War","option_b":"Gaza conflict","option_c":"Ukraine War","option_d":"Myanmar civil war","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The Ukraine War was the world's deadliest conflict in 2025 with almost 78,000 battle-related and 2,000+ civilian deaths (Armed Conflict Location & Event Project—ACLED, US dataset). Top 10 deadliest conflicts globally: Ukraine (78,000), Sudan (60,000+), Israel-Palestine (30,000+), Myanmar (9,000+), Nigeria (8,000+), Somalia (5,000+), Syria (4,000+), Mexico (3,000+), Brazil (2,500+), DRC (2,000+). Ukraine's lethality reflected: (a) sustained state-on-state warfare (conventional militaries, not insurgency); (b) Russian indiscriminate targeting of Ukrainian civilian areas (Kharkiv, Lviv, Odesa bombardments killing 2,000+); (c) attritional tactics (trench warfare, WW1-style casualties); (d) advanced weapons usage (drones, cruise missiles, artillery); (e) scale of forces engaged (1.5+ million combatants). Russia's strategy shifted from quick-victory blitzkrieg (February 2022) to grinding attrition, resulting in 500,000+ cumulative casualties (Russian + Ukrainian combined, killed + wounded). The Ukraine War's intensity exceeded recent conflicts: Syria 11-year civil war (500,000 deaths), Yemen (300,000+), Afghanistan (20 years, 200,000+). For India: the Ukraine War highlighted the costs of prolonged state conflict in the modern era and provided strategic lessons on: (a) defensive advantages (Ukraine 'holding' despite being outgunned initially); (b) asymmetric warfare effectiveness (drones, MANPADS vs. armor); (c) international support importance (NATO weapons, intelligence); (d) civilian resilience under bombardment. The 78,000 figure represented only counted deaths; total mortality (including famine, disease, displacement) was estimated 2-3x higher.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22046,"question_text":"US-Ukraine-Russia trilateral talks were announced at WEF Davos 2026 to begin in which city?","option_a":"Geneva","option_b":"Istanbul","option_c":"Abu Dhabi","option_d":"Vienna","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"At WEF Davos 2026, Zelenskyy met Trump and announced US-Ukraine-Russia trilateral peace talks to begin in Abu Dhabi on January 23, 2026. Abu Dhabi (UAE) was chosen as a neutral location. The talks followed multiple failed ceasefire attempts. US Secretary of State Rubio acknowledged negotiations had 'stagnated.'","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22047,"question_text":"In the Russia-Ukraine conflict, Ukraine has used which NATO nation's F-16 fighter jets, delivered in 2024-2025?","option_a":"USA directly","option_b":"Poland and Czech Republic","option_c":"Netherlands and Denmark (transferred with US approval)","option_d":"Germany and France","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Netherlands and Denmark transferred F-16 fighter jets to Ukraine (with US approval) in 2024-2025 — a major boost to Ukraine's air force. The F-16s required Ukrainian pilot training in Romania. Germany supplied Leopard 2 tanks; UK supplied Storm Shadow cruise missiles. Russia condemned F-16 deliveries as NATO escalation.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22048,"question_text":"Russia's war in Ukraine began on February 24, 2022. How many years had the conflict lasted by May 2026?","option_a":"2 years","option_b":"3 years","option_c":"4 years, 3 months","option_d":"5 years","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Russia's full-scale invasion of Ukraine began February 24, 2022. By May 2026, the conflict had lasted over 4 years and 3 months. The conflict was preceded by Russia's annexation of Crimea (2014) and support for Donbas separatists. By 2025, it was the world's deadliest conflict with 78,000+ fatalities.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22049,"question_text":"NATO pledged €40 billion in defense aid to Ukraine in 2025 (at The Hague Summit). What controversial issue regarding Ukraine's NATO membership remained unresolved?","option_a":"Language requirements for Ukrainian military","option_b":"Ukraine's nuclear disarmament","option_c":"No NATO membership pathway, timeline, or invitation offered","option_d":"Ukraine's constitution amendment requirement","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Despite €40 billion in aid, NATO's Hague Summit 2025 offered Ukraine NO membership invitation, NO timeline, NO significant upgrade in membership language — deeply disappointing Kyiv. NATO's position was that Ukrainian NATO membership must be considered after the war ends. Meanwhile, NATO's defense spending was raised to 5% GDP.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22050,"question_text":"The Gaza ceasefire 'Phase 1' was agreed on January 15, 2025. Who mediated this ceasefire?","option_a":"USA alone","option_b":"Qatar, Egypt, and USA","option_c":"UN and Arab League","option_d":"Turkey and Iran","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The Gaza ceasefire Phase 1 (January 15, 2025) was mediated by Qatar, Egypt, and the USA — the same troika that mediated all Gaza ceasefire talks. Phase 1 involved a 42-day pause and hostage releases. The conflict had started on October 7, 2023 with Hamas's attack on Israel. By 2025, Gaza rebuilding faced immense challenges.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22051,"question_text":"Which country was described as having the 'world's worst humanitarian crisis' in terms of absolute numbers of displaced persons by mid-2025?","option_a":"Syria","option_b":"Ukraine","option_c":"Sudan","option_d":"Myanmar","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Sudan's civil war (started April 2023: SAF vs RSF) caused the 'world's worst humanitarian crisis' by mid-2025 — 10 million internally displaced + 3 million fled to neighboring countries; 2/3 of Sudan's 50 million people needed assistance. RSF captured El Fasher (North Darfur capital) on October 27, 2025 after an 18-month siege.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22052,"question_text":"Sudan's civil war (2023-2025) is between the Sudanese Armed Forces (SAF) and which rebel group?","option_a":"SPLM/A-North","option_b":"Rapid Support Forces (RSF)","option_c":"Justice and Equality Movement (JEM)","option_d":"Sudan Liberation Army","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Sudan's civil war: SAF (Sudanese Armed Forces) vs. RSF (Rapid Support Forces) — started April 2023. RSF was led by Mohamed Hamdan Dagalo ('Hemeti'). RSF captured El Fasher in October 2025. Sudan's conflict was rated the most likely to escalate in 2026 by International Crisis Group. Casualty estimates: 150,000+ killed.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},

    # GLOBAL GEOPOLITICS 2025-2026 (22053-22066)
    {"id":22053,"question_text":"On January 3, 2026 the US launched 'Operation Absolute Resolve' against which country, capturing its sitting president?","option_a":"Cuba — Miguel Díaz-Canel captured","option_b":"Venezuela — Nicolás Maduro captured","option_c":"Nicaragua — Daniel Ortega captured","option_d":"Bolivia — Luis Arce captured","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Operation Absolute Resolve (January 3, 2026, pre-dawn coordinated assault)—the Trump administration's direct military intervention in Venezuela, capturing sitting President Nicolás Maduro and extracting him to New York for federal indictment on narcotics trafficking, corruption, money laundering charges. The operation employed ~150 US combat aircraft (F-22 Raptors, F-35 Lightning IIs, B-1 Lancers, F-16s), establishing air superiority, followed by special operations forces (SOCOM Delta Force, Navy SEAL Team 6) insertion into Miraflores Palace (presidential residence) in Caracas. The operation lasted 18 minutes, with Maduro subdued and flown via US aircraft to Guantanamo Bay, then to New York federal courthouse. The capture of a sitting head of state and extrajudicial transfer to foreign courts violated multiple international law principles: UN Charter Article 2(4) (prohibition on use of force), UN General Assembly resolutions on sovereignty, and established norms against 'regime change' operations (post-2003 Iraq War international consensus). Venezuela's opposition leader María Corina Machado (Nobel Peace Prize 2025 winner) was subsequently sworn in as interim president with US recognition. Venezuela holds 303 billion barrels of proven oil reserves (20% of global reserves), making the resource-control dimension controversial. For India: the operation raised strategic concerns about US willingness to unilaterally intervene in sovereign states (applicable to India-Pakistan disputes, China-Taiwan, etc.), setting precedent for unilateral power projection. The operation destabilized regional oil markets, raised crude prices, and displaced Venezuelan refugees (500,000+ asylum-seekers). The legality under UN Charter was contested; China, Russia, Iran, and Non-Aligned Movement states condemned it as imperialism. The capture marked a return to 19th-century 'gunboat diplomacy' after 75 years of post-WWII UN Charter restraint.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22054,"question_text":"Myanmar's civil war since 2021 has caused how many deaths and displacements by 2025?","option_a":"10,000 deaths, 500,000 displaced","option_b":"50,000+ deaths (per ACLED estimates), 3+ million internally displaced and 1 million fled","option_c":"20,000 deaths, 1 million displaced","option_d":"150,000 deaths, 5 million displaced","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Myanmar's civil war (February 1, 2021 coup by General Min Aung Hlaing onward): approximately 50,000+ deaths per ACLED (Armed Conflict Location & Event Data Project) estimates (battle-related + extrajudicial killings), 3+ million internally displaced, 1+ million fled abroad (Bangladesh 1M+, Thailand 100K+, India 50K+) by 2025. The ACLED estimates, refined through the conflict's 5-year trajectory, indicate ~50,000 casualties rather than earlier high-end projections. The conflict is described as 'under the radar' globally relative to Ukraine, Iran, Gaza—despite higher intensity (2021-2025 comparable to Ukraine War casualty rate per capita). ASEAN's Five-Point Consensus (April 2021) calling for dialogue, non-interference, and military aid cessation remained largely unimplemented. Myanmar's junta, backed by Russia and China, resisted ASEAN mediation. Key combatants: Myanmar Military (Tatmadaw) vs. 150+ ethnic armed organizations (EAOs: Karen National Liberation Army, Shan State Army, Arakan Army) plus People's Defence Force (PDF, anti-junta militia). The conflict involves: (a) ethnic separatism (Karen, Shan, Arakan, Rakhine autonomy demands); (b) post-coup democratic resistance (civil disobedience, armed rebellion); (c) humanitarian crisis (2M+ food insecure, 50%+ health system collapse). For India: Myanmar's instability threatened India's Northeast region (Mizoram, Nagaland) via cross-border rebel bases; India's regional influence in Myanmar declined post-coup; BIMSTEC economic integration stalled. Bangladesh's refugee absorption from Myanmar (1M+ Rohingya + Myanmar protesters) created burden-sharing pressures. At ASEAN KL 2025, leaders reviewed Myanmar's crisis but achieved no breakthrough. Min Aung Hlaing was 'elected' president in April 2026 in a junta-managed election—formalizing the coup's consolidation. International sanctions (US, EU, UK) had minimal impact on junta's behavior. The conflict represented the largest humanitarian crisis in Southeast Asia post-1990s Cambodia.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22055,"question_text":"Russia was the FIRST country to officially recognise the Taliban government of Afghanistan. When did this happen?","option_a":"January 2024","option_b":"July 3, 2025","option_c":"March 2025","option_d":"October 2025","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Russia became the FIRST country to officially recognize the Taliban government of Afghanistan on July 3, 2025 — breaking a 3.5-year international consensus of non-recognition. Russia established formal diplomatic relations with the Taliban-led Islamic Emirate of Afghanistan. This was seen as Russia's strategy to expand influence in Central Asia.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22056,"question_text":"The Houthi group (Yemen) has been led by which figure, conducting attacks on Red Sea shipping since 2023?","option_a":"Ali Abdullah Saleh","option_b":"Mohammed al-Houthi","option_c":"Abdul-Malik al-Houthi","option_d":"Hassan al-Houthi","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Abdul-Malik al-Houthi leads the Houthi movement (Ansar Allah) in Yemen since 2003 (founding) and expanded to national insurgency post-2015 Saudi coalition intervention. Since October 7, 2023 (Hamas-Israel war start), Houthis conducted systematic attacks on Red Sea commercial shipping via anti-ship missiles (Yemeni-modified cruise missiles, range 1,500+ km), naval mines, and unmanned boats—claiming 'solidarity with Gaza' and targeting US-allied shipping. The attacks disrupted 12-20% of global shipping through the Red Sea/Bab el-Mandeb Strait (34 km chokepoint carrying $700B+ annual trade). Houthis hit 100+ vessels by May 2026. US-UK Operation Poseidon Archer (January-May 2025) conducted airstrikes on Houthi military targets (drone bases, missile sites, ports in Hodeidah) but failed to deter attacks. The campaign reflected Houthis' asymmetric strategy: low-cost drones/missiles vs. expensive destroyers providing escorts (cost asymmetry of 1:100). For global trade: Red Sea disruption forced rerouting around Africa's Cape of Good Hope, adding USD 200,000+ per container and 10-14 voyage days. Insurance premiums tripled. For India: Houthi attacks threatened Indian shipping (5,000+ Indian vessels transiting Red Sea annually); Indian Navy provided escorts to Indian merchant vessels; oil import costs surged (India imports 70% crude from Middle East). Houthis received funding and weapons from Iran's IRGC Quds Force (Iran-backed proxy network). Yemen's civil war (SAF vs. STC vs. Houthis, 2015-2025) killed 300,000+; Houthis controlled 70% of population (northwest Yemen). The Red Sea disruption persisted until May 2026, causing estimated USD 10 billion in additional global shipping costs.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22057,"question_text":"The New START nuclear arms control treaty between USA and Russia expired in February 2026. What was Russia's decision regarding the treaty?","option_a":"Russia agreed to extend for 5 more years","option_b":"Russia suspended participation in 2023 and treaty lapsed","option_c":"Russia signed a replacement treaty with China","option_d":"Russia unilaterally withdrew in 2025","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Russia suspended participation in the New Strategic Arms Reduction Treaty (New START, signed Prague April 2010 by Obama-Medvedev) on February 21, 2023 (citing US support for Ukraine as pretext). The treaty capped US and Russian deployed strategic nuclear warheads at 1,550 each, deployed delivery systems (ICBMs, SLBMs, strategic bombers) at 700, and total warheads at 6,000 each—the last bilateral binding nuclear arms control agreement post-Cold War. The treaty lapsed in February 2026 when it expired, leaving no nuclear arms control framework between the two largest nuclear powers (Russia 5,459 warheads, USA 5,177) for the first time since 1972 (SALT-I era). The lapse created strategic ambiguity: (a) unknown force levels (Russia may have deployed 2,000+ warheads vs. treaty's 1,550 cap); (b) no verification mechanisms (IAEA inspections ceased); (c) destabilized mutual deterrence (both sides feared surprise disarming strike capability). For nuclear-armed India: the lapse illustrated risks of bilateral arms control collapse—relevant to India-Pakistan nuclear standoff stability. India had advocated for US-Russia nuclear talks as global security imperative at the UN. The lapse also accelerated China's nuclear modernization (estimated 500+ warheads by 2030) due to perceived US-Russia disarmament inequities. Trump administration (2025-2026) showed no interest in reviving New START, instead pursuing unilateral advantages. Pakistan's nuclear arsenal (170-180 warheads) benefited from reduced superpower oversight, complicating India-Pakistan deterrence calculations. The New START lapse marked the end of 50+ years of bilateral strategic arms control architecture.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22058,"question_text":"'Operation Poseidon Archer' was conducted by which countries against Houthi targets in Yemen in 2025?","option_a":"USA and Israel","option_b":"USA and UK","option_c":"Saudi Arabia and UAE","option_d":"USA, UK, and France","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Operation Poseidon Archer: US and UK conducted airstrikes against Houthi (Ansar Allah) military targets in Yemen in response to Houthi attacks on Red Sea commercial shipping. Houthis targeted ships claiming solidarity with Gaza. The Red Sea disruptions cost global shipping tens of billions in additional costs (rerouting via Cape of Good Hope).","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22059,"question_text":"The IRGC (Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps) is Iran's elite military force. Which component specifically manages Iran's missile and drone programs?","option_a":"IRGC Ground Forces","option_b":"IRGC Aerospace Force","option_c":"Quds Force","option_d":"IRGC Navy","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"IRGC Aerospace Force manages Iran's ballistic missile and drone programs — including the Shahed-136 suicide drones and Fateh/Zulfiqar ballistic missiles. IRGC Aerospace Force Commander Amir Ali Hajizadeh was killed in the Twelve-Day War (June 2025). Quds Force (external operations) is a separate IRGC branch. IRGC is designated a terrorist organization by USA.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22060,"question_text":"Which of the following is the correct IRGC component that conducts Iran's foreign proxy operations?","option_a":"IRGC Aerospace Force","option_b":"IRGC Ground Forces","option_c":"IRGC Quds Force","option_d":"IRGC Navy","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"IRGC Quds Force conducts Iran's external operations — supporting proxies including Hezbollah (Lebanon), Hamas (Gaza), Houthis (Yemen), and PMF (Iraq). Qasem Soleimani, legendary Quds Force commander, was killed by US drone strike on January 3, 2020 at Baghdad airport. His successor, Esmail Qaani, became Quds Force commander.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22061,"question_text":"Which country in the Middle East hosts the largest US air base (Al Udeid) that was struck by Iran during the 2026 Iran War?","option_a":"Bahrain","option_b":"Saudi Arabia","option_c":"Qatar","option_d":"Kuwait","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Al Udeid Air Base — the US's largest Middle East air base — is in Qatar, hosting ~10,000 US personnel and the US Air Forces Central Command (AFCENT). Qatar also hosts the HQ of Hamas political bureau. Iran struck Al Udeid during both the Twelve-Day War (June 2025) and the 2026 Iran War. Qatar also mediated the Gaza ceasefire.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22062,"question_text":"The 'Axis of Resistance' — Iran's network of regional proxies — includes which of the following groups?","option_a":"Hezbollah, Hamas, Houthis, and PMF (Iraqi militias)","option_b":"Al-Qaeda, ISIS, Taliban, and Hezbollah","option_c":"Hamas, PLO, Fatah, and PA","option_d":"Al-Shabaab, Boko Haram, and Hezbollah","correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Iran's 'Axis of Resistance': Hezbollah (Lebanon), Hamas (Gaza), Houthis/Ansar Allah (Yemen), and PMF (Popular Mobilization Forces) in Iraq. These groups receive funding, weapons, and training from Iran's IRGC Quds Force. The 2026 Iran War significantly degraded the Axis of Resistance as Iran's own military was severely weakened.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22063,"question_text":"Iran's Natanz nuclear facility is located in which Iranian province?","option_a":"Tehran Province","option_b":"Bushehr Province","option_c":"Isfahan Province","option_d":"Khuzestan Province","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Natanz uranium enrichment facility is in Isfahan Province, Iran — about 250 km south of Tehran. It is Iran's main uranium enrichment site with thousands of centrifuges. Isfahan also hosts the UCF (Uranium Conversion Facility). Both Natanz and Isfahan were targeted by Israel in the Twelve-Day War (June 2025). Fordow is in Qom Province.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22064,"question_text":"India's strategic interest in the 2026 Iran War context involves which Iranian port under India's development?","option_a":"Bandar Abbas","option_b":"Chabahar Port","option_c":"Kish Island","option_d":"Bushehr Port","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Chabahar Port (Sistan-Baluchistan Province, southeastern Iran, 600 km from Hormuz Strait on the Gulf of Oman) is India's strategic infrastructure investment—India has invested $500+ million in developing it (Phase 1: 2016-2022 completion; Phase 2: 2023-2030 expansion). Chabahar's strategic value: (a) provides India access to Afghanistan (Zaranj-Delaram highway) and Central Asia (bypassing Pakistan's blockade); (b) reduces India's Malacca Strait dependency (via Cape, around Malacca takes 40 days); (c) enables Indian maritime presence in Arabian Sea; (d) facilitates India-Iran-Afghanistan trade corridor (IIAC). During the 2026 Iran War (Feb-April 2026), India maintained strict neutrality—refusing to condemn Operation Epic Fury or support sanctions—partly to protect Chabahar from collateral war damage or Iranian retaliation. Indian Navy vessels at Chabahar were mobilized to: (a) prevent Chabahar's military targeting (de facto protection); (b) maintain supply lines to Indian nationals evacuated via Operation Swades; (c) monitor Hormuz closure impacts on Indian shipping. Diplomatically, External Affairs Minister S. Jaishankar met Iranian FM Araghchi on May 15, 2026 (post-ceasefire), reaffirming India's commitment to Chabahar despite war disruptions. Operationally, the 2026 war forced Chabahar into partial shutdown (3-week closure, restarted at 40% capacity by May). For India's strategic autonomy: Chabahar represents India's independent infrastructure corridor outside China's Belt-and-Road Initiative (BRI) and US sanctions architecture—a counterweight to Gwadar Port (Pakistan-China). India's 8 million nationals in Gulf region faced evacuation risks during the war; Chabahar remained India's principal evacuation hub alongside Bandar Abbas. The port's disruption threatened India's $2.5 billion annual Afghanistan trade and 2,000+ Indian workers' employment.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22065,"question_text":"The Samson Option refers to Israel's:","option_a":"Conventional military doctrine of preemptive strikes","option_b":"Massive nuclear retaliation deterrence strategy","option_c":"Intelligence infiltration of enemy states","option_d":"Iron Dome missile defense protocol","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The Samson Option is Israel's undeclared nuclear deterrence strategy — the threat of massive nuclear retaliation if Israel's existence is threatened (named after the biblical Samson who destroyed himself and his enemies). Israel maintains a policy of 'nuclear ambiguity' (neither confirming nor denying nuclear weapons). SIPRI estimates Israel has ~90 nuclear warheads.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22066,"question_text":"Which country has the 'Iron Dome,' 'Arrow,' and 'David's Sling' missile defense systems — all activated during the Twelve-Day War 2025?","option_a":"Saudi Arabia","option_b":"UAE","option_c":"Israel","option_d":"USA","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Israel operates a multi-layer missile defense: Iron Dome (short-range, 4-70 km rockets/mortars), David's Sling (medium-range, 40-300 km), and Arrow 2/3 (long-range, 100-2,400 km, ballistic missiles). During the Twelve-Day War (June 2025), all three layers were activated against Iran's Operation True Promise III (550+ ballistic missiles + 1,000+ drones). US also provided Patriot interceptors.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},

    # HIGHER ORDER / MIXED (22067-22080)
    {"id":22067,"question_text":"Which statement about India-Pakistan 2025 conflict is CORRECT?","option_a":"India attacked Pakistan's regular army bases","option_b":"Operation Sindoor targeted only JeM and LeT terrorist infrastructure","option_c":"The ceasefire was mediated by China","option_d":"Pakistan launched its operation before India did","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Operation Sindoor (May 7, 2025) specifically targeted terrorist infrastructure of JeM and LeT — NOT Pakistani military or civilian targets. India emphasized this distinction. Pakistan's Operation Bunyan-um-Marsoos was a retaliation AFTER India's Sindoor. The ceasefire was reached on May 10, 2025, with US Secretary of State Rubio involved diplomatically.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22068,"question_text":"Which of the following chronological sequences of Iran-related conflicts is CORRECT?","option_a":"True Promise I → Twelve-Day War → True Promise III → 2026 Iran War","option_b":"True Promise I (Apr 2024) → True Promise II (Oct 2024) → Twelve-Day War (Jun 2025) → 2026 Iran War (Feb 2026)","option_c":"Twelve-Day War → True Promise III → True Promise I → 2026 Iran War","option_d":"True Promise I → True Promise III → Twelve-Day War → 2026 Iran War","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Correct sequence: Operation True Promise I (Iran's first direct attack on Israel, April 13-14, 2024) → Operation True Promise II (Iran's second attack, October 2024) → Twelve-Day War / Operation True Promise III (June 13-24, 2025) → 2026 Iran War (February 28, 2026 - ongoing with Pakistan-mediated ceasefire April 8, 2026).","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22069,"question_text":"The JCPOA (Joint Comprehensive Plan of Action) — Iran nuclear deal — was signed in 2015. When did the USA withdraw?","option_a":"2016 under Obama","option_b":"2018 under Trump","option_c":"2020 under Trump","option_d":"2022 under Biden","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The JCPOA (Iran nuclear deal, P5+1 + EU + Iran) was signed in 2015 under Obama. Trump withdrew the USA in 2018 ('maximum pressure' policy). Iran then gradually exceeded JCPOA limits — enriching to 60% uranium purity (near weapons-grade of 90%). Biden tried but failed to revive JCPOA. JCPOA collapse led to escalation culminating in the Twelve-Day War (2025) and 2026 Iran War.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22070,"question_text":"Which Swiss think tank assessed India's air superiority in Operation Sindoor forced Pakistan to seek ceasefire — and which institution tracks global nuclear arsenals?","option_a":"CSS ETH Zürich (air superiority); SIPRI (nuclear arsenals)","option_b":"IISS (air superiority); SIPRI (nuclear arsenals)","option_c":"Chatham House (air superiority); IAEA (nuclear arsenals)","option_d":"Rand Corporation (air superiority); UN (nuclear arsenals)","correct_answer":"A","explanation":"CSS ETH Zürich (Center for Security Studies at Swiss Federal Institute of Technology) assessed India's air superiority in Operation Sindoor. SIPRI (Stockholm International Peace Research Institute) tracks global nuclear arsenals — as of 2025: Russia 5,459 warheads, USA 5,177, China ~500, Pakistan ~170, India ~172, Israel ~90, North Korea ~50.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22071,"question_text":"The 2026 Iran War (started Feb 28, 2026) was described as starting DURING which process?","option_a":"Iran-Saudi normalization talks","option_b":"Iran-US nuclear negotiations","option_c":"IAEA inspection visit","option_d":"UN Security Council deliberations","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The 2026 Iran War notably began on February 28, 2026 DURING active Iran-US nuclear negotiations — making the timing highly controversial. Critics argued the attack violated diplomatic norms. The US and Israel launched coordinated strikes killing Supreme Leader Khamenei and multiple senior officials. Iran accused the US of 'bad faith' in negotiations.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22072,"question_text":"India's stance during the Iran-US-Israel conflict was characterized as:","option_a":"Strong support for USA and Israel","option_b":"Support for Iran","option_c":"Neutral — engaging diplomatically with both sides","option_d":"Complete non-engagement","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"India maintained a neutral stance during the Iran-US-Israel conflict — using diplomacy to engage both sides. EAM Jaishankar met Iranian FM Araghchi in New Delhi (May 15, 2026) while India also maintained defense cooperation with Israel. India's interests: Chabahar Port (Iran), 8 million Indian diaspora in Gulf, energy imports. India abstained from UN votes on the conflict.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22073,"question_text":"Which is the CORRECT match of military operation → date?","option_a":"Operation Sindoor → April 22, 2025","option_b":"Twelve-Day War → June 13-24, 2025","option_c":"2026 Iran War began → March 15, 2026","option_d":"Gaza Phase 1 ceasefire → March 2025","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Correct: Twelve-Day War → June 13-24, 2025 ✓. Incorrect: Operation Sindoor → May 7, 2025 (not April 22 which was the Pahalgam attack), 2026 Iran War → February 28, 2026 (not March), Gaza Phase 1 ceasefire → January 15, 2025 (not March). Key dates to remember for AP HC exam.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22074,"question_text":"Pakistan mediated the April 8, 2026 Iran-US ceasefire. What motivated Pakistan to take this role despite being under nuclear tension with India?","option_a":"Pakistan is a NATO ally","option_b":"Pakistan has historically mediated all Middle East conflicts","option_c":"Pakistan shares borders with Iran and has common Muslim identity plus interest in regional stability","option_d":"China directed Pakistan to mediate","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Pakistan mediated the Iran-US ceasefire partly because: Pakistan shares a 900 km border with Iran, has common Muslim identity and historical ties, and benefits from regional stability (energy imports, trade). Pakistan also sought to project global diplomatic relevance after the India-Pakistan standoff of May 2025 weakened its international image.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22075,"question_text":"Sudan's civil war (SAF vs RSF): Which major city fell to the RSF on October 27, 2025, after an 18-month siege?","option_a":"Khartoum","option_b":"Omdurman","option_c":"El Fasher","option_d":"Port Sudan","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"El Fasher — capital of North Darfur state and the last major city under government (SAF) control in Darfur — fell to the RSF (Rapid Support Forces) on October 27, 2025, after an 18-month siege. This was a major RSF victory, extending their control over most of Darfur. ICG rated Sudan as the most likely conflict to escalate in 2026.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22076,"question_text":"The Ukraine war saw Trump broker a 3-day ceasefire (May 9-11, 2026). US Secretary of State Rubio described the peace process as having:","option_a":"Made significant progress with a permanent deal imminent","option_b":"Stagnated — US mediation has not led to a fruitful outcome","option_c":"Successfully achieved permanent ceasefire terms","option_d":"Resulted in China joining as co-mediator","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"US Secretary of State Marco Rubio said US mediation efforts on Ukraine have 'stagnated' and have not led to a 'fruitful outcome.' This contrasted with Trump's optimism ('the beginning of the end'). Putin suggested the war was 'coming to an end' (May 2026). The Institute for the Study of War (ISW) noted that ceasefires without enforcement mechanisms are unlikely to hold.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22077,"question_text":"Russia officially recognized the Taliban government in Afghanistan on July 3, 2025 — becoming the first country to do so. What was Russia's strategic motivation?","option_a":"Expanding military bases in Afghanistan","option_b":"Countering US influence and expanding Russian influence in Central Asia","option_c":"Trade in Afghan lithium resources","option_d":"Russian Orthodox Church expansion in Afghanistan","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Russia recognized the Taliban (July 3, 2025) primarily to expand Russian influence in Central Asia and counter US interests. Russia sees Afghanistan as key to Central Asian geopolitics. Russia also wanted to prevent Afghanistan from becoming a safe haven for anti-Russian groups, and sought economic benefits including trade routes and energy projects.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22078,"question_text":"Israel's PM during the Twelve-Day War (2025) and 2026 Iran War was:","option_a":"Naftali Bennett","option_b":"Yair Lapid","option_c":"Benny Gantz","option_d":"Benjamin Netanyahu","correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Benjamin Netanyahu was Israel's PM during both the Twelve-Day War (June 2025) and the 2026 Iran War. Israel's key leaders: Netanyahu (PM), Israel Katz (Defense Minister), Eyal Zamir (IDF Chief of Staff). Netanyahu had been indicted by ICC in November 2024 for alleged war crimes in Gaza. He remained in office despite legal challenges.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22079,"question_text":"The CENTCOM (US Central Command) commander during the 2026 Iran War was:","option_a":"General Kenneth McKenzie","option_b":"General Lloyd Austin","option_c":"General Michael Kurilla","option_d":"General Mark Milley","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"General Michael Kurilla commanded CENTCOM (US Central Command — responsible for Middle East, Central Asia, South Asia) during the 2026 Iran War. US chain of command: Pete Hegseth (Defense Secretary) → Dan Caine (Joint Chiefs Chairman) → Michael Kurilla (CENTCOM). CENTCOM HQ: Tampa, Florida with forward HQ at Al Udeid, Qatar.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22080,"question_text":"The Houthi attacks on Red Sea shipping (2023-2025) primarily targeted ships going through which waterway, disrupting global trade? [2025-2026 Rewrite v2]","option_a":"Suez Canal","option_b":"Red Sea and Bab el-Mandeb Strait","option_c":"Persian Gulf","option_d":"Gulf of Aden only","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Houthis targeted commercial shipping in the Red Sea and Bab el-Mandeb Strait — between Yemen (Africa-Arabian Peninsula) — disrupting 12-15% of global trade. Ships rerouted around Africa's Cape of Good Hope, adding 10-14 days and $1M+ per voyage. The Red Sea connects Suez Canal to Indian Ocean. Operation Poseidon Archer (US-UK) struck Houthi targets in Yemen in response.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},

    # ── 2026 FRESHNESS GAP-FILL: IDs 22081-22102 (added May 19, 2026) ──
    # OPERATION EPIC FURY / IRAN CRISIS 2026 (22081-22084)
    {"id":22081,"question_text":"What was the official US-Israel codename for the February 28, 2026 coordinated strike on Iran that killed Supreme Leader Khamenei?","option_a":"Operation Rising Lion","option_b":"Operation Epic Fury","option_c":"Operation Midnight Hammer","option_d":"Operation True Promise IV","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Operation Epic Fury — coordinated US-Israel strike launched February 28, 2026 on Iran's leadership, nuclear, and missile infrastructure. Ayatollah Ali Khamenei (Supreme Leader since June 4, 1989) was killed on Day 1; former Majlis Speaker Ali Larijani also killed. The strike occurred DURING active US-Iran nuclear negotiations, generating diplomatic controversy. Followed by the Pakistan-mediated April 8 conditional ceasefire and failed Islamabad talks (Apr 11-12).","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22082,"question_text":"Just before Operation Epic Fury, India launched 'Operation Swades' to evacuate ~10,000 Indians from Iran. What primarily triggered the evacuation?","option_a":"Israeli airstrikes on Tehran","option_b":"Mass civil unrest + economic collapse (rial at 42,000/USD, inflation 42%) from Dec 28, 2025","option_c":"Iranian government deportation order","option_d":"Earthquake in northern Iran","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Operation Swades (Dec 2025-Jan 2026) was triggered by Iran's economic implosion — rial in free-fall (~42,000/USD), inflation breaching 42%, mass unrest erupting Dec 28, 2025. ~10,000 Indian nationals were airlifted via IAF C-17 Globemaster & C-130J Super Hercules plus Indian Navy ships from Chabahar/Bandar Abbas. It preceded the Feb 28, 2026 US-Israel Operation Epic Fury.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22083,"question_text":"Ali Larijani, killed in Operation Epic Fury (Feb 28, 2026), held which prior position in Iran's political system?","option_a":"Foreign Minister","option_b":"Speaker of the Majlis (Iranian Parliament)","option_c":"Chief Justice","option_d":"Defense Minister","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Ali Larijani was a former Speaker of the Majlis (Iranian Parliament, 2008-2020) and a powerful conservative voice in the Islamic Republic. He was killed alongside Supreme Leader Ali Khamenei on Day 1 of Operation Epic Fury (Feb 28, 2026). The strike effectively decapitated Iran's senior political-religious leadership, on top of the military commanders already killed in the June 2025 Twelve-Day War.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22084,"question_text":"The Pakistan-hosted US-Iran talks at Islamabad (April 11-12, 2026) collapsed primarily over which set of disputes?","option_a":"Hostage exchange and prisoner lists","option_b":"US bases withdrawal demand, permanent nuclear dismantlement, $270B-$1T Iran reparations claim","option_c":"Hajj quota and oil pricing","option_d":"Strait of Hormuz transit fees","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The Islamabad talks (April 11-12, 2026) — convened after the April 8 conditional ceasefire — collapsed over three issues: (a) Iran's demand for full US withdrawal from Persian Gulf bases; (b) US insistence on permanent dismantlement of Iran's nuclear program; (c) Iran's $270 billion to $1 trillion reparations/damages claim from Operation Epic Fury destruction. Pakistan-mediated.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},

    # ISRAEL-LEBANON 2026 + GAZA PEACE COUNCIL (22085-22089)
    {"id":22085,"question_text":"The Israel-Hezbollah War 2026 (parallel to the Iran War) erupted on which date?","option_a":"February 28, 2026","option_b":"March 2, 2026","option_c":"April 8, 2026","option_d":"May 7, 2026","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The Israel-Hezbollah / Israel-Lebanon War 2026 erupted on March 2, 2026 — concurrent with the Iran War (Operation Epic Fury, Feb 28). 2,000+ killed in Lebanon; 1,000-1,900 Hezbollah fighters killed. Washington DC talks held April 14-15, 2026 leading to a 10-day ceasefire announced April 17, 2026. Israel struck Beirut again on May 7, 2026 killing the then-current Hezbollah leader.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22086,"question_text":"The Israel-Lebanon 10-day ceasefire was announced on April 17, 2026 after talks held in which city?","option_a":"Doha","option_b":"Cairo","option_c":"Washington DC","option_d":"Riyadh","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The Israel-Lebanon ceasefire talks were held in Washington DC on April 14-15, 2026, leading to a 10-day ceasefire announced on April 17, 2026. The talks ran in parallel with the Pakistan-mediated Iran-US Islamabad talks (Apr 11-12 — which collapsed). Both arose from the same Feb 28, 2026 Israel-Iran escalation that engulfed Lebanon from March 2, 2026.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22087,"question_text":"The Gaza Peace Council was inaugurated on January 22, 2026 at WEF Davos. Who chairs it?","option_a":"UN Secretary-General António Guterres","option_b":"US President Donald Trump","option_c":"Saudi Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman","option_d":"Egyptian President Abdel Fattah el-Sisi","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The Gaza Peace Council was inaugurated on January 22, 2026 at the 56th WEF Davos by US President Donald Trump, who chairs it. UNSC had authorised it on November 17, 2025. Director-General: Nickolay Mladenov (former UN Special Coordinator for Middle East Peace). Members include UAE, Saudi Arabia, Pakistan. India, China and Russia abstained, citing the lack of direct Palestinian Authority participation.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22088,"question_text":"Which countries notably abstained from joining the Gaza Peace Council (inaugurated Jan 22, 2026)?","option_a":"UK, France, Germany","option_b":"India, China, Russia","option_c":"Japan, South Korea, Australia","option_d":"Brazil, South Africa, Mexico","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"India, China and Russia abstained from joining the Gaza Peace Council, citing sovereignty concerns and the need for direct Palestinian Authority participation. India's abstention reflected its long-standing two-state stance and its 1974 status as the first non-Arab country to recognise the PLO. UAE, Saudi Arabia, Pakistan and other US-aligned Sunni-Arab states joined.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22089,"question_text":"On March 30, 2026, Israel's Knesset passed which controversial law targeting Palestinian convicts?","option_a":"Lifetime exile to a third country","option_b":"Mandatory death penalty for Palestinians convicted of attacks on Israeli citizens","option_c":"Collective family deportation","option_d":"Stripping of residency rights","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"On March 30, 2026, the Knesset passed a law imposing the MANDATORY death penalty on Palestinians convicted of attacking Israeli citizens — a sharp escalation from Israel's earlier discretionary use of capital punishment. The law came amid the Israel-Lebanon War and faced strong international criticism. Israel had also demolished the UNRWA HQ in East Jerusalem's Sheikh Jarrah neighbourhood in February 2026.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},

    # US GLOBAL OPS 2025-26 (22090-22092)
    {"id":22090,"question_text":"On December 26, 2025, Israel became the FIRST country in the world to recognise which long-unrecognised African breakaway state?","option_a":"Western Sahara","option_b":"Somaliland","option_c":"Puntland","option_d":"South Ossetia","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"On December 26, 2025, Israel became the first country to officially recognise Somaliland — which had declared independence from Somalia on May 18, 1991 (after fall of Siad Barre) but had spent 35 years unrecognised. Strategic rationale: Berbera Port on the Gulf of Aden / Red Sea — countering Houthi maritime threats and Bab el-Mandeb security. Ethiopia had earlier signed a 2024 MoU for Berbera sea access.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22091,"question_text":"'Operation Hunter' (January 10, 2026) was a US military operation in which country, conducted in cooperation with that country's new post-Assad government?","option_a":"Iraq — al-Sudani govt","option_b":"Lebanon — Berri govt","option_c":"Syria — Ahmed al-Shara govt","option_d":"Yemen — Mashat govt","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Operation Hunter (Jan 10, 2026) — US strikes on ISIS strongholds in Syria, conducted in cooperation with the new Syrian government of President Ahmed al-Shara (HTS-led, in power since Bashar al-Assad's ouster in December 2024). It was part of a Trump 2.0 'hard power before diplomacy' sequence: Dec 25-26 Nigeria/Sokoto strikes vs Boko Haram + ISIS-WA; Dec 30 Saudi strikes on Mukulla port (Yemen); Jan 3 Operation Absolute Strike (Venezuela); Jan 10 Operation Hunter (Syria).","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22092,"question_text":"Saudi Arabia's December 30, 2025 air strikes on which Yemeni port targeted the Southern Transitional Council (STC) forces?","option_a":"Aden","option_b":"Hodeidah","option_c":"Mukulla","option_d":"Mocha","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"On December 30, 2025, Saudi Arabia conducted air strikes on Mukulla port (Yemen) against Southern Transitional Council (STC) forces — a Houthi-related escalation in Yemen's long-running multi-sided civil war. The Saudi action came alongside US-UK Operation Poseidon Archer (against Houthis in north-west Yemen) and reflected the fragmentation of Yemeni anti-Houthi forces.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},

    # TRUMP ISOLATIONISM (22093-22094)
    {"id":22093,"question_text":"On January 7, 2026, President Trump signed an executive order withdrawing the US from how many international bodies in a single day — the largest such withdrawal in US history?","option_a":"12","option_b":"34","option_c":"66","option_d":"112","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"On January 7, 2026, Trump's executive order withdrew the US from 66 international bodies simultaneously — the largest single-day multilateral withdrawal in US history. Notable exits: International Solar Alliance (ISA, India-France led), IPCC, UN ECOSOC, UNFPA. Earlier exits (since Jan 20, 2025): WHO and Paris Agreement (Day 1), UNHRC, UNESCO. This dismantled US presence in global climate and development governance.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22094,"question_text":"Trump's January 2026 Greenland annexation overtures provoked a joint condemnation from how many EU countries plus the UK?","option_a":"3 EU countries + UK","option_b":"8 EU countries + UK","option_c":"15 EU countries + UK","option_d":"All 27 EU countries + UK","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Trump's January 2026 push to annex Greenland (a Danish autonomous territory of strategic Arctic value — minerals + sea routes) drew a joint condemnation from 8 EU countries plus the UK. Trump retaliated with a 10% additional tariff on countries opposing the annexation talks. The Trans-Atlantic deal froze on Jan 13, 2026 after a 25-minister meeting failed.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},

    # PARIS COALITION + UKRAINE 2026 (22095-22096)
    {"id":22095,"question_text":"The 'Coalition of the Willing' on Ukraine security guarantees was convened in Paris on January 6, 2026 — led by which leader, after Trump tilted toward bilateral Russia talks?","option_a":"German Chancellor Friedrich Merz","option_b":"French President Emmanuel Macron","option_c":"UK PM Keir Starmer","option_d":"Polish PM Donald Tusk","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"French President Emmanuel Macron led the Paris 'Coalition of the Willing' on January 6, 2026 to provide security guarantees to Ukraine — building an alternative architecture as the US under Trump pursued a bilateral track with Russia. Participants: France, UK, Germany, Italy, Poland, Nordics, Baltics, Canada, Australia. Absent: USA, Hungary (Orbán still PM at the time), Slovakia.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22096,"question_text":"The New START treaty — the last US-Russia nuclear arms control treaty — expired on which date in 2026, leaving no binding strategic arms cap for the first time since 1972?","option_a":"January 1, 2026","option_b":"February 5, 2026","option_c":"April 10, 2026","option_d":"May 5, 2026","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"New START expired on February 5, 2026 — for the first time since 1972 (SALT-I era), no binding US-Russia strategic arms control treaty exists. Signed at Prague (April 2010) by Obama and Medvedev, it had capped each side at 1,550 deployed strategic warheads + 700 deployed delivery systems. Russia had suspended participation in Feb 2023. Restart talks held in Abu Dhabi (UAE-mediated) produced no breakthrough.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},

    # SOUTH ASIA POLITICAL TURMOIL 2026 (22097-22100)
    {"id":22097,"question_text":"Bangladesh held its first general election after Sheikh Hasina's August 2024 ouster on February 18, 2026. Which party won, and how many of 300 seats?","option_a":"Awami League — 175 seats","option_b":"Jatiya Party — 120 seats","option_c":"BNP (Bangladesh Nationalist Party) — 209 seats","option_d":"Jamaat-e-Islami — 95 seats","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Bangladesh Nationalist Party (BNP) won a landslide victory of 209 of 300 seats (70% majority) in the February 18, 2026 general election—Bangladesh's first election after the August 5, 2024 ouster of PM Sheikh Hasina (after 16 years in office). Awami League (AL) was BANNED from contesting—the first exclusion of a major party from elections since Bangladesh's 1971 independence—citing AL's responsibility for the July 2024 Gen-Z-led mass uprising that killed ~1,400 protesters in police crackdowns (July 16-August 5). The interim government, led by Muhammad Yunus (Nobel Prize 2006, microfinance pioneer), managed 6-month democratic transition. BNP's victory reflected: (a) anti-Hasina backlash (corruption allegations, 16 years authoritarian rule, extrajudicial killings); (b) pro-Islamic voter sentiment (BNP's Jamaat-e-Islami ally received ministerial positions); (c) younger generation's demand for democratic accountability. For India: the BNP victory raised concerns—BNP historically tilt pro-Pakistan, advocate closer Islamic ties, and oppose India-centric policies. Key India-Bangladesh issues: (a) Hindu minority protection (Hindus 8% of population, ~2,600 documented attacks post-Hasina ouster); (b) Teesta water-sharing treaty (pending 20+ years); (c) border terrorism via Rohingya camps; (d) maritime boundary disputes (Saint Martin Island). BNP's coalition with Jamaat-e-Islami (designated terrorist by India for 2008 Mumbai attack links) alarmed New Delhi. India's response: cautious engagement, monitoring minority rights, securing maritime boundaries. Bangladesh's transition from Hasina's quasi-authoritarian rule to BNP's Islamic-tilted democracy represented a significant shift in South Asian geopolitics.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22098,"question_text":"Who was sworn in as Bangladesh's new Prime Minister on February 17, 2026 — the son of late PM Khaleda Zia and BNP founder Ziaur Rahman?","option_a":"Sheikh Hasina","option_b":"Khaleda Zia","option_c":"Tarek Rahman","option_d":"Muhammad Yunus","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Tarek Rahman was sworn in as Bangladesh's new PM on February 17, 2026 by President Mohammed Shahabuddin. Son of Khaleda Zia (late former PM) and Ziaur Rahman (BNP founder & former President), he had been in UK exile since 2008. His prior in-absentia convictions were overturned by the Yunus interim government. India's concerns: Hindu minorities (~8% pop., 2,600+ documented attacks since Aug 2024), BNP's traditional pro-Pakistan tilt, Teesta water-sharing agreement.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22099,"question_text":"Min Aung Hlaing was elected President of Myanmar on April 9, 2026 in a junta-managed election. How many votes did he secure out of 584?","option_a":"289","option_b":"345","option_c":"429","option_d":"512","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Min Aung Hlaing—the junta chief (Senior General) who orchestrated Myanmar's February 1, 2021 coup (claiming election fraud despite Aung San Suu Kyi's NLD's landslide 2020 victory)—was 'elected' President with 429 of 584 votes in a heavily controlled April 9, 2026 'election.' Yi Win was appointed new Army Chief, consolidating junta succession. The election was widely condemned as illegitimate: (a) Parliament dominated by military-appointed members (25% reserved seats) and Union Solidarity and Development Party (USDP, military's civilian façade); (b) opposition parties (NLD) banned; (c) no international observers; (d) opposition leaders (Aung San Suu Kyi, Win Myint) imprisoned for politically-motivated charges; (e) voting in war zones under military control impossible due to active combat. Myanmar's civil war (Feb 2021-May 2026): 90,000+ battle deaths, 3+ million internally displaced, 1+ million fled abroad. Opposing forces: Tatmadaw (junta military, 400,000+ troops), PDF (People's Defence Force, anti-junta militia, 100,000+ fighters), 150+ ethnic armed organizations. For India: Myanmar's junta 'election' indicated consolidation of Beijing-backed authoritarian rule (Min Aung Hlaing visiting Beijing multiple times since 2021), exclusion of India from Myanmar's strategic calculus, and deterioration of India-Myanmar relations post-coup. India's BIMSTEC coordination collapsed. Northeast India border security threats persisted due to rebel bases in Myanmar. The 2026 'election' formally ended any international pretense of legitimate governance transition—Myanmar joined authoritarian axis (Russia, Belarus, North Korea). International sanctions (US, EU, UK, Canada) remained ineffective; Myanmar's isolation deepened, pushing it fully into Chinese orbit (BRI, strategic dependence).","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22100,"question_text":"Nepal's 47th Prime Minister Balendra Shah ('Balen') was sworn in on March 27, 2026 at age 35 — Nepal's youngest ever PM. The previous day's protests against KP Sharma Oli's September 2025 crackdown reportedly killed how many?","option_a":"12","option_b":"38","option_c":"76","option_d":"150","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Balen Shah — civil engineer + rapper + former Kathmandu Mayor — sworn in as Nepal's 47th PM on March 27, 2026 at 35 (youngest ever). The September 2025 Gen-Z protests (sparked by a social media ban and economic stagnation) faced lethal force authorised by then-PM KP Sharma Oli; 76 were killed in the crackdown. Oli was arrested on March 28, 2026 (the day after Balen's swearing-in).","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},

    # INDIA NEIGHBOURHOOD + LAC 2026 (22101-22102)
    {"id":22101,"question_text":"India formally objected on January 9, 2026 to Chinese infrastructure construction in which Pakistan-Occupied territory ceded by Pakistan to China under the 1963 Sino-Pakistan Boundary Agreement?","option_a":"Aksai Chin","option_b":"Shaksgam Valley (5,180 sq km)","option_c":"Gilgit-Baltistan","option_d":"Trans-Karakoram Tract — Hunza","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"India formally objected on January 9, 2026 to Beijing's infrastructure construction in the Shaksgam Valley (Trans-Karakoram Tract)—5,180 sq km of erstwhile Jammu & Kashmir territory that Pakistan illegally ceded to China under the March 2, 1963 Sino-Pakistan Boundary Agreement (signed between Chinese Foreign Minister Zhou Enlai and Pakistani FM Z.A. Bhutto). India rejects the 1963 agreement as null and void—same legal position applied to China-Pakistan Economic Corridor (CPEC) infrastructure in Gilgit-Baltistan. The 1963 agreement was signed without India's consent despite J&K's legal sovereignty (India-China war 1962 context prevented India's participation). Shaksgam's cession involved 5,180 sq km of high-altitude trans-Himalayan plateau (3,600+ m elevation, uninhabited, glaciated) connecting Xinjiang to Gilgit. China's infrastructure projects (post-2024 LAC disengagement accord) included: roads, surveillance posts, communication networks, suggesting Beijing's preparation for long-term occupation consolidation. India's January 2026 objection reaffirmed: (a) India's constitutional claim to J&K (Constitution Article 370); (b) rejection of unilateral territorial cession by Pakistan (which India treats as incomplete/provisional); (c) India's commitment to resolving territorial disputes bilaterally via Simla Agreement (1972) framework, not third-party involvement. The objection occurred despite India-China October 2024 LAC disengagement (Depsang, Demchok) and 2025 Modi-Xi handshake at Kazan BRICS Summit—indicating India's strategic differentiation: LAC peace vs. broader territorial claim maintenance. Pakistan's 1963 cession was Pakistan's attempt to resolve Sino-Pakistani disputes (similar to 1949 Karachi Agreement with China on border) while facing India's superior claims under UN's decolonization principle (India's J&K inheritance from British-era princely states).","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":22102,"question_text":"India-China border trade resumed via the Lipulekh Pass (Uttarakhand) in April 2026 — ending a freeze that had lasted how many years?","option_a":"24 years (since 2002)","option_b":"40 years","option_c":"64 years (since the 1962 Sino-India War)","option_d":"75 years","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"India-China border trade via the Lipulekh Pass (Uttarakhand, 3,680 m elevation, Kumaon Himalayas) resumed in April 2026—ending a 64-year freeze dating back to the October-November 1962 Sino-Indian War (NEFA campaign, Ladakh front). The Lipulekh trade route historically connected Garhwal (India) to Tibet (Kailas, Mansarovar region) via ancient salt/wool caravan networks; closure following the 1962 war represented the most prolonged closure of India-China border trade in modern history. The April 2026 resumption was symbolic reconstruction of diplomatic normalization following: (a) October 2024 LAC (Line of Actual Control) disengagement agreement on Depsang and Demchok sectors (de-escalation of forward positions, disputed since 2020 Galwan clash); (b) May 2025 Modi-Xi handshake at Kazan BRICS Summit (first positive engagement post-2020); (c) Resolution of bilateral military tensions (withdrawal of force concentrations, de-escalation protocols). The trade resumption was accompanied by: (a) reopening of Kailash Mansarovar Yatra (pilgrimage route via Lipulekh, suspended 2020-2024, ~5,000 annual pilgrims); (b) institutional frameworks (Joint Working Group on borders, confidence-building measures); (c) economic spillovers (limited, ~USD 136B bilateral trade remains far below potential due to persistent border disputes). Operationally, the April 2026 opening involved: reconstruction of 40 km of trade route via Lipulekh, establishment of border customs posts, and phased merchant traffic (initial 100 traders/month). Politically, the resumption signaled India-China's managed rivalry model—compartmentalizing economic cooperation while maintaining security standoff on disputed borders (Arunachal Pradesh, Ladakh, Sikkim). The Kailash Mansarovar resumption particularly held religious-cultural significance: the Mansarovar lake sacred in Hinduism, Buddhism, and Jainism; pilgrimage cessation had angered Indian Hindu constituencies. The trade's symbolic and economic insignificance (compared to 2020-2024 military tensions) suggested border normalization remained fragile—contingent on sustained diplomatic management.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
]


